#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172985} :activity/id #uuid "1bda195a-6aa9-5403-8c13-a26311503b1d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/id #uuid "cc0e7551-dafb-5e86-9c0e-20d3207eccdf" :topic/math-academy-id 3344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171889 :question/id #uuid "f0111439-4fd8-5add-b95f-5e783178fd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-9440"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417343}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "8de1d301-7c80-5374-bd47-c7e3b3821626" :step/math-academy-id 35366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172084 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171890 :question/id #uuid "8f9615ef-b53e-51ee-9aa6-4df88afd6773" :question/math-academy-id "e-9441"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380650}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "7533639d-bfe8-58f0-b64f-5c767246da78" :step/math-academy-id 35367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173197 :tutorial/content "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ assigns a vector to every point $(x,y)$ in a region $S\\subset \\mathbb R^2$. We can visualize this by plotting the corresponding vector at each point in the coordinate plane.\n\nAs an example, let's sketch the vector field $\\mathbf F$ at the points $(1,1), (-3,3),$ and $(4,-3),$ where $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j}.$$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we first substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =i+j \\\\ F(-3,3) & =-3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ F(4,-3) & =4\\,i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors, placing the tail ends of each vector at its corresponding point. This gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/da/daea999f1a9bff587f0550e6674a39ae27dc9a2bb58e35877ed15626de0d9791.png)\n\nApplying the same method to more points in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we obtain the following visualization of our vector field:\n\n![](images/03/03402a7a7e1967b428c3657d6be90e4b1fd7ddd3c8ccaa11a69b0ff7cd566859.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ed5e3d1-f4e2-5773-bbd0-5ef02d8b726d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5763 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f845bde7-8e1b-5e89-8f3e-66a8c7e912eb" :step/math-academy-id 35369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171894 :question/id #uuid "b226c391-9538-59ff-85ff-dde4d73f209b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9473"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375804}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "64cb83b5-85c0-5f79-8253-5fd52adedcd8" :step/math-academy-id 35450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173198 :tutorial/content "Given a visualization, it's possible to determine characteristics about the underlying vector field.\n\nFor example, let's consider the visualization below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e3ecb8b2d64aef1cf4654fea8e3ad8f176e9d830ee8b402a281155c3b4ef645.png)\n\nThis visualization was generated by one of the following functions.\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y) = y \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\nWe can determine which function generated the above diagram using a process of elimination:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. This means we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf j$ component is negative for some $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$$\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. This means that the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is dependent on the variable $x.$\n\nSo, we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf i$ component is independent of $x.$ Hence, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf H(x,y) = y\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "372dc560-67d1-5888-9353-8f8f365cff15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5773 :tutorial/title "Analyzing Vector Field Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "51e7b312-b65a-5a9c-88d2-2bb1e9411b96" :step/math-academy-id 35467 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172984}}] :activity/title "Visualizing Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172171}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417344} :activity/id #uuid "6f1cb101-0015-5868-a6fa-64d8ac5ce0c8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/id #uuid "cc0e7551-dafb-5e86-9c0e-20d3207eccdf" :topic/math-academy-id 3344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417344 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af393b5-0b0a-51ee-963e-9232b624b8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253755} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebe45a6-6f14-5783-a2c5-0fff74a623ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254352} {:answer/id #uuid "5a235ec0-b137-57da-bcd7-fa7e7f95a670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254460} {:answer/id #uuid "6c27c496-3691-52cb-89b6-a510f5ec6427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254631} {:answer/id #uuid "99ad8cef-0db5-5109-bbb4-eb38f07c8dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254631} :answer-field/id #uuid "1226930a-6b24-57fb-a5e7-1829039d9728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "505eb075-afb5-5225-b846-447773bcc89d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122559" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+j+k,\\,\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=i.$"} :step/id #uuid "0dc29e20-9d3a-5e3d-9c90-7b8b29d2ac4f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417345 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e846d3-cfa4-53b9-966f-e4adab0cb729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1d/1d502f64bd89571fcd7ccb43e339b0fb8554ebf2072d48eaf10829f96f9c170e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417331} {:answer/id #uuid "acf72fee-ab09-5c2f-a567-2d99703c6cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ea/eac1fe8c8dee7394a49603187829adcc0fa81665e0eafb2674f43ebdf4493f10.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417334} {:answer/id #uuid "b10e879c-3c4a-5dd0-9361-bbc17fe37c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5d/5d27f4544c601da995ad2c1402547a477a244a6b79bff4e8b79f945299b73b87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417335} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5ff7a9-5c75-54e7-82a4-5ae809e4f3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e40599ebb674fe5fe05c60be75f148f41458469ae6926c3388675ecdb24f66cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417337} {:answer/id #uuid "d640a56e-f8d2-517d-a8ab-e60c9f56c036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/59/59bf689a32a8a0c5d347f29065acea60194b9f4b0fad084108ecb657257cd939.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417337} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10e9eb4-ee70-5020-8593-2fc167326803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d04f0b91-140d-549f-8f9d-31d1d9b6c7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122758" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,4),(2,-2),$ and $(4,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-x\\,i-y\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,4) & =-(1)\\,i-4\\,j=-i-4\\,j \\\\ F(2,-2) & =-(2)\\,i-(-2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(4,-2) & =-(4)\\,i-(-2)\\,j=-4\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/e4/e40599ebb674fe5fe05c60be75f148f41458469ae6926c3388675ecdb24f66cf.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1b8d0142-f4e6-56fc-84f8-a8543a949309" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba98b8c-2116-50e2-b2de-6aae7dbae22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254058} {:answer/id #uuid "735ac856-ee4b-5c8a-9daf-474103fa33a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254692} {:answer/id #uuid "a2214b75-4ff9-5986-b2e2-09f3f7eea2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255100} {:answer/id #uuid "e51e621f-adf6-56d4-ba3a-23552d243dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255694} {:answer/id #uuid "fce86be7-59a1-5b1f-98d2-ae97d81427f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254692} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1d0012-9007-5d62-a389-7be8930ea3d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5e48f80-38d5-59fb-be5e-a4668cd85716" :question/math-academy-id "q-122632" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a032eb49f78ce7bd54de6969f030e4316f2bd3028280f73991af3fe45521733c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i+j,\\,K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=5\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=x\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "5abc9e5b-dfec-5521-ae1a-20eaaff9c78a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417348}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44832c8e-8988-5ee0-ac8b-67ae439d923f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257381} {:answer/id #uuid "70a0b147-68a3-5ec0-ae90-b9c82057aad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} {:answer/id #uuid "96d0c94c-faa8-5d39-b3c3-d13382b0eced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258114} {:answer/id #uuid "acdf99e3-cf16-5362-88ce-860873156446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258289} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d361e0-0169-5758-b933-86a6b5b677fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-y\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} :answer-field/id #uuid "861c8be9-902d-5545-831a-501704ab8be1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c63175a-a455-54b8-b784-f9c30e4507e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123748" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bff60466ca8abba4fd849c046c02af3a4811279774975fc981cafad91d734ed5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-i+j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i.$"} :step/id #uuid "6b4f8a97-87e5-50b3-a283-1ef6e69a5e50"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52540afe-6bae-5db6-95a2-a9867a8f858b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} {:answer/id #uuid "52693f37-1a7d-5506-8ee4-6500aa2d7349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417333} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ec2c00-c765-5813-aaa4-789423b68551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417336} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c9d1db-461c-5c21-9d19-a6e9f1533a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5ef832-2edf-5758-ba7c-c1096ad3a8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ffaea-faee-50f8-bb72-c0bdccf87c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "548bbae5-603d-5bc6-9f7d-4ca6dbc971eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122670" :question/problem "![](images/64/64f02fa7dcd23b20e61801c0453d8fe7ed013936572e1ee63f748361edd4dec5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i,\\,G(x,y,z)=-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."} :step/id #uuid "80d8fd6a-6a07-5ff4-8ccd-33cb7f7a2af9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417345}}] :activity/title "Visualizing Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417330}]}
